<article xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="review-article" dtd-version="1.3"><?properties open_access?><processing-meta base-tagset="archiving" mathml-version="3.0" table-model="xhtml" tagset-family="jats"><restricted-by>pmc</restricted-by></processing-meta><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">JACC Asia</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">JACC Asia</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>JACC Asia</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="epub">2772-3747</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Elsevier</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta>
<article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">41198371</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">12673831</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">S2772-3747(25)00535-6</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.jacasi.2025.08.027</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>State-of-the-Art Review</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Epidemiology of Myocarditis in Young and Middle-Aged Population in Asia</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au1"><name><surname>Li</surname><given-names>Chenze</given-names></name><degrees>MD, PhD</degrees><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">a</xref><xref rid="aff2" ref-type="aff">b</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au2"><name><surname>Lu</surname><given-names>Zhibing</given-names></name><degrees>MD, PhD</degrees><email>luzhibing222@163.com</email><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">a</xref><xref rid="aff2" ref-type="aff">b</xref><xref rid="cor2" ref-type="corresp">&#x02217;</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au3"><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>Dao Wen</given-names></name><degrees>MD, PhD</degrees><email>dwwang@tjh.tjmu.edu.cn</email><xref rid="aff3" ref-type="aff">c</xref><xref rid="cor1" ref-type="corresp">&#x02217;</xref></contrib><aff id="aff1"><label>a</label>Department of Cardiology, Zhongnan Hospital of Wuhan University, Wuhan, Hubei, China</aff><aff id="aff2"><label>b</label>Institute of Myocardial Injury and Repair, Hubei Provincial Clinical Research Center for Cardiovascular Intervention, Wuhan University, Wuhan, Hubei, China</aff><aff id="aff3"><label>c</label>Division of Cardiology, Department of Internal Medicine, Tongji Hospital, Tongji Medical College, Huazhong University of Science and Technology, Hubei Key Laboratory of Genetics and Molecular Mechanisms of Cardiological Disorders, Wuhan, Hubei, China</aff></contrib-group><author-notes><corresp id="cor1"><label>&#x02217;</label><bold>Address for correspondence:</bold> Dr Dao Wen Wang, Division of Cardiology, Department of Internal Medicine, Tongji Hospital, Tongji Medical College, Huazhong University of Science &#x00026; Technology, 1095# Jiefang Avenue, Wuhan 430030, China. <email>dwwang@tjh.tjmu.edu.cn</email></corresp><corresp id="cor2"><label>&#x02217;</label>Dr Zhibing Lu, Department of Cardiology, Zhongnan Hospital of Wuhan University, Wuhan University, 169# Donghu Road, Wuhan 430071, China. <email>luzhibing222@163.com</email></corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="pmc-release"><day>04</day><month>11</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><!-- PMC Release delay is 0 months and 0 days and was based on <pub-date
						pub-type="epub">.--><pub-date pub-type="collection"><month>11</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>04</day><month>11</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><volume>5</volume><issue>11</issue><fpage>1399</fpage><lpage>1414</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>23</day><month>7</month><year>2025</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>7</day><month>8</month><year>2025</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; 2025 The Authors</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2025</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/).</license-p></license></permissions><abstract id="abs0010"><p>Myocarditis, characterized by inflammation of the myocardium and subsequent injury of cardiac function, has garnered increasing attention among the young and middle-aged population in Asia. Its epidemiology is influenced by a combination of environmental factors, genetic predisposition, and socioeconomic status. Global disease burden analyses indicate that the prevalence of myocarditis varies by region, with certain areas in Asia reporting higher rates. However, the true burden is likely underestimated due to clinical underdiagnosis and inadequate health surveillance. Regarding prognosis, most acute cases may recover, but persistent subclinical abnormalities often remain. Notably, the most severe form, fulminant myocarditis, is associated with a high in-hospital mortality rate when treated conventionally. Nevertheless, early implementation of a life support&#x02013;based treatment can significantly improve outcomes. This review focuses on summarizing the epidemiology of myocarditis in the young and middle-aged Asian population, aiming to provide a theoretical foundation for enhancing the management of myocarditis in this demographic in the future.</p></abstract><abstract abstract-type="graphical" id="abs0015"><title>Central Illustration</title><fig id="undfig1" position="anchor"><graphic xlink:href="ga1"/></fig></abstract><abstract abstract-type="author-highlights" id="abs0020"><title>Highlights</title><p><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0010"><list-item id="u0010"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0010">The epidemiology of myocarditis in the young and middle-aged population within Asia remains poorly characterized.</p></list-item><list-item id="u0015"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0015">This review summarizes the existing data on the burden, diagnosis, management, and long-term outcomes of myocarditis in the young and middle-aged population in Asia.</p></list-item><list-item id="u0020"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0020">Current data indicate regional and age-related disparities in the epidemiology of myocarditis, highlighting the need for tailored preventive strategies, early diagnosis, and appropriate management of this condition.</p></list-item></list></p></abstract><kwd-group id="kwrds0010"><title>Key Words</title><kwd>Asia</kwd><kwd>epidemiology</kwd><kwd>myocarditis</kwd><kwd>young and middle-aged population</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta><def-list><title>Abbreviations and Acronyms</title><def-item><term id="kwrd0035">CMR</term><def><p>cardiac magnetic resonance imaging</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="kwrd0045">GBD</term><def><p>Global Burden of Disease</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="kwrd0055">ICI</term><def><p>immune checkpoint inhibitor</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="kwrd0065">LVAD</term><def><p>left ventricular assist device</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="kwrd0075">sST2</term><def><p>soluble suppression of tumorigenicity-2</p></def></def-item></def-list></front><body><p id="p0025">Myocarditis, an inflammatory disease of the myocardium and subsequent injury of cardiac function, is characterized by heterogeneous clinical manifestations ranging from subclinical presentations to life-threatening hemodynamic compromise or sudden cardiac death.<xref rid="bib1" ref-type="bibr"><sup>1</sup></xref> Its pathogenesis involves the infections of viruses or other pathogens, autoimmune responses, or drug-induced toxicity.<xref rid="bib2" ref-type="bibr"><sup>2</sup></xref> The long-term implications for the young and middle-aged population are particularly concerning, as myocarditis represents a leading cause of dilated cardiomyopathy and sudden cardiac death in this demographic.<xref rid="bib3" ref-type="bibr"><sup>3</sup></xref> Although 70% of acute cases may spontaneously recover ventricular function, subclinical abnormalities in diastolic function and regional strain can persist.<xref rid="bib4" ref-type="bibr"><sup>4</sup></xref> Notably, a significant proportion of patients with myocarditis can develop progressive dilated or inflammatory cardiomyopathy, necessitating advanced therapies including transplantation or ventricular assist devices.<xref rid="bib5" ref-type="bibr"><sup>5</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib6" ref-type="bibr"><sup>6</sup></xref></p><p id="p0030">Currently, most existing reviews on myocarditis primarily focus on the Western population. The epidemiology of myocarditis in the young and middle-aged population within Asia remains poorly characterized. In the Asian population, the epidemiological landscape presents distinct characteristics shaped by demographic, genetic, environmental, and health care&#x02013;related factors. First, Asia&#x02019;s vast population significantly amplifies the absolute burden of myocarditis. According to data from the Global Burden of Disease (GBD) Collaborative Network,<xref rid="bib7" ref-type="bibr"><sup>7</sup></xref> the estimated number of incident myocarditis in 2021 was approximately 778,590 cases in Asia, which is substantially higher than in Europe (187,856 cases), Africa (155,453 cases), and the Americas (195,876 cases). Moreover, the number of deaths due to myocarditis in 2021 was also higher in Asia (22,138 cases) compared with Europe (6,166 cases), Africa (1,639 cases), and the Americas (1,768 cases). Second, genetic diversity among Asian ethnic groups influences susceptibility and disease progression. For instance, polymorphisms in the human leukocyte antigen and major histocompatibility complex gene, which modulate immune responses to viral triggers such as coxsackievirus B3 and susceptibility to autoimmune myocarditis, exhibit varied prevalence across different populations.<xref rid="bib8" ref-type="bibr"><sup>8</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib9" ref-type="bibr"><sup>9</sup></xref> Furthermore, environmental exposures endemic to Asia, such as high rates of dengue infections, also correlate with the incidence rate of myocarditis.<xref rid="bib10" ref-type="bibr"><sup>10</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib11" ref-type="bibr"><sup>11</sup></xref></p><p id="p0035">In this review, we aimed to address critical knowledge gaps by synthesizing existing data on the incidence, clinical profiles, and long-term outcomes of myocarditis in the Asian young and middle-aged population. By integrating these findings, this work seeks to provide a theoretical foundation for improving the management of myocarditis in this population.</p><sec id="sec1"><title>Global and Asian Burden of Myocarditis</title><p id="p0040">The global burden of myocarditis exhibits notable regional and age-related disparities, with emerging data highlighting differences between Western and Asian populations, as well as distinct patterns among young and middle-aged adults vs other age groups. A study analyzing the global burden of myocarditis from 1990 to 2021 revealed that the age-standardized incidence rate of myocarditis in Southeast Asia was approximately 17.87 (95% CI: 14.46-21.9) per 100,000 persons, whereas in the high-income Asia Pacific, it was approximately 19.36 (95% CI: 15.94-23.71) per 100,000 persons. Both rates exceeded the global average of 16.16 (95% CI: 13.11-19.76) per 100,000 persons. Furthermore, the disability-adjusted life years rate of myocarditis in Central Asia was approximately 27.41 (95% CI: 21.93-34.56) per 100,000 persons, and in East Asia, it was approximately 25.02 (95% CI: 17.86-30.91) per 100,000 persons, both of which were above the global average of 12.41 (95% CI: 10.37-14.76) per 100,000 persons. The age-standardized death rate of myocarditis in Central Asia was approximately 0.84 (95% CI: 0.68-1.05) per 100,000 persons, and in East Asia, it was approximately 0.91 (95% CI: 0.63-1.18) per 100,000 persons, also exceeding the global average of 0.40 (95% CI: 0.32-0.47) per 100,000 persons. Moreover, this study indicated that the young and middle-aged population carried a predominant burden of disability-adjusted life years, with the exception of children younger than 5 years old.<xref rid="bib12" ref-type="bibr"><sup>12</sup></xref> For other age-stratified analyses, studies also revealed a pronounced burden among young and middle-aged patients, who accounted for 65% of myocarditis cases in both Western registries and Asian series.<xref rid="bib13" ref-type="bibr"><sup>13</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib14" ref-type="bibr"><sup>14</sup></xref> These regional- and age-specific patterns underscore the need for tailored preventive algorithms and therapeutic protocols to address epidemiological heterogeneity in global myocarditis management.</p><p id="p0045">To provide more straightforward data on the burden of myocarditis in Asia, the authors obtained relevant information from the GBD Collaborative Network.<xref rid="bib15" ref-type="bibr"><sup>15</sup></xref> This review graphically presents the original data available in this open database, which offers detailed information on the incidence and mortality of myocarditis rates across Asia. It should be noted that the data presented does not involve any in-depth statistical analysis. Furthermore, the classification of Asian regions and countries is consistent with the reports from an official United Nations definition.<xref rid="bib16" ref-type="bibr"><sup>16</sup></xref> The rates presented are crude rates, rather than age-standardized rates, thereby accurately reflecting the true burden of myocarditis, as discussed in the study by Zhao.<xref rid="bib17" ref-type="bibr"><sup>17</sup></xref>
<xref rid="fig1" ref-type="fig">Figure 1</xref> shows that from 1990 to 2021, the crude incidence rate of myocarditis exhibited an upward trend in Asia. The crude incidence rate of myocarditis in 1990 was 14.32 (95% CI: 11.53-17.72) per 100,000 persons, which increased to 16.84 (95% CI: 13.60-20.77) per 100,000 persons by 2021. In terms of the crude mortality rate, a significant increase was observed from 1990 to 2002, followed by no significant change from 2002 to 2021. The crude mortality rate in 1990 was 0.42 (95% CI: 0.33-0.53) per 100,000 persons, rising to 0.48 (95% CI: 0.35-0.57) per 100,000 persons in 2021. Throughout the years, male individuals exhibited higher crude incidence and mortality rates than female individuals. In addition, we present the incidence and mortality rates across different regions in Asia, as illustrated in <xref rid="fig2" ref-type="fig">Figures 2</xref> and <xref rid="fig3" ref-type="fig">3</xref>. The data revealed that Japan had the highest incidence rates in both 1990 (22.67; 95% CI: 18.10-28.04 per 100,000 persons) and 2021 (31.52; 95% CI: 24.74-38.85 per 100,000 persons), whereas Western Asia demonstrated relatively lower incidence rates, ranging from 8.24 to 17.08 per 100,000 persons in 1990 to 8.94 to 21.10 per 100,000 persons in 2021. Regarding mortality, significant changes were noted between 1990 and 2021. Sri Lanka reported the highest mortality rate in 1990 (1.72; 95% CI: 1.02-2.27 per 100,000 persons); however, by 2021, Kazakhstan (2.34; 95% CI: 1.76-3.16 per 100,000 persons) emerged as the country with the highest mortality rate in Asia, despite its relatively low mortality rate in 1990 (0.08; 95% CI: 0.06-0.12 per 100,000 persons). The study from Li et al<xref rid="bib12" ref-type="bibr"><sup>12</sup></xref> indicated that from 1990 to 2021, Kazakhstan experienced the highest growth rate of 13.64% in the age-standardized mortality rate of myocarditis.<fig id="fig1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Crude Incidence Rates and Mortality Rates From 1990 to 2021 in Asia</p><p>(A) Crude incidence rates from 1990 to 2021. (B) Crude mortality rates from 1990 to 2021. The data were sourced from the Global Burden of Disease Study Database.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="gr1"/></fig><fig id="fig2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Crude Incidence Rates of Myocarditis Across Asian Countries</p><p>(A) Crude incidence rates across Asian countries in 1990. (B) Crude incidence rates across Asian countries in 2021. The data were sourced from the Global Burden of Disease Study Database.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="gr2"/></fig><fig id="fig3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Crude Mortality Rates of Myocarditis Across Asian Countries</p><p>(A) Crude mortality rates across Asian countries in 1990. (B) Crude mortality rates across Asian countries in 2021. The data were sourced from the Global Burden of Disease Study Database.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="gr3"/></fig></p><p id="p0050">The proportions of incident myocarditis cases and deaths in 2021 are also summarized in <xref rid="fig4" ref-type="fig">Figures 4</xref> and <xref rid="fig5" ref-type="fig">5</xref>, using data from the GBD database. The findings indicated that the incident myocarditis cases were predominantly higher among the young and middle-aged population, accounting for 51.64% of the total cases in Asia. The proportions of the young and middle-aged population in Central Asia, Western Asia, Southern Asia, Eastern Asia, and Southeastern Aisa were 60.15%, 60.90%, 57.52%, 43.68%, and 55.92%, respectively. Furthermore, myocarditis-related deaths were most prevalent among the young and middle-aged demographic in Central Asia and Southern Asia, representing 55.20% and 42.19% of the total, respectively. In addition, <xref rid="tbl1" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref> presents all published studies on myocarditis within the Asian population, each with a sample size exceeding 200 cases.<xref rid="bib11" ref-type="bibr"><sup>11</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib13" ref-type="bibr"><sup>13</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib18" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>, <xref rid="bib19" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>, <xref rid="bib20" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>, <xref rid="bib21" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="bib22" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>, <xref rid="bib23" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>, <xref rid="bib24" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>, <xref rid="bib25" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>, <xref rid="bib26" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>, <xref rid="bib27" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>, <xref rid="bib28" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>, <xref rid="bib29" ref-type="bibr">29</xref>, <xref rid="bib30" ref-type="bibr">30</xref> In these studies, the cohorts primarily consisted of young and middle-aged patients, with a higher prevalence of male patients compared with female patients. These data reflect that the young and middle-aged population is the most frequently affected, underscoring the need for more management strategies tailored for this demographic.<fig id="fig4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Proportions of Incident Myocarditis Cases by Age Groups</p><p>(A) In Asia, (B) in Central Asia, (C) in Western Asia, (D) in Southern Asia, (E) in Eastern Asia, and (F) in Southeastern Asia. The data were sourced from the 2021 Global Burden of Disease Study Database.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="gr4"/></fig><fig id="fig5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Proportions of Myocarditis Deaths by Age Groups</p><p>(A) In Asia, (B) in Central Asia, (C) in Western Asia, (D) in Southern Asia, (E) in Eastern Asia, and (F) in Southeastern Asia. The data were sourced from the 2021 Global Burden of Disease Study Database.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="gr5"/></fig><table-wrap position="float" id="tbl1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Summary of Published Studies on Myocarditis in the Asian Population With Sample Sizes &#x0003e;200 Cases</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th rowspan="2">First Author</th><th rowspan="2">Publication Year</th><th rowspan="2">Country</th><th rowspan="2">Study Population</th><th rowspan="2">Sample Size</th><th rowspan="2">Age, y</th><th rowspan="2">Male, %</th><th colspan="2">In-Hospital<hr/></th><th colspan="4">Follow-Up<hr/></th></tr><tr><th>Mortality, %</th><th>Heart Failure, %</th><th>Time</th><th>Heart Transplantation, %</th><th>Heart Failure, %</th><th>Cardiovascular Death, %</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Isogai et al<xref rid="bib19" ref-type="bibr"><sup>19</sup></xref></td><td align="center">2015</td><td align="center">Japan</td><td>Fulminant myocarditis with mechanical circulatory support</td><td align="center">603</td><td align="center">16-69 accounted for 79.6%</td><td align="center">58.7</td><td align="center">35.3</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td></tr><tr><td>Li et al<xref rid="bib11" ref-type="bibr"><sup>11</sup></xref></td><td align="center">2016</td><td align="center">China</td><td>Dengue with myocarditis</td><td align="center">201</td><td align="center">63 &#x000b1; 21</td><td align="center">58.7</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">25.4</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td></tr><tr><td>Te et al<xref rid="bib20" ref-type="bibr"><sup>20</sup></xref></td><td align="center">2017</td><td align="center">China</td><td>Myocarditis</td><td align="center">13,250</td><td align="center">43 &#x000b1; 27</td><td align="center">47.1</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">10.4 &#x000b1; 2.9 y</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">6.5</td></tr><tr><td>Chang et al<xref rid="bib21" ref-type="bibr"><sup>21</sup></xref></td><td align="center">2017</td><td align="center">China</td><td>Adult patients with acute myocarditis who admitted to ICU</td><td align="center">1145</td><td align="center">40 &#x000b1;15</td><td align="center">59.3</td><td align="center">14.5</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">14 y</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">8.2</td><td align="center">13.8</td></tr><tr><td>Xu et al<xref rid="bib22" ref-type="bibr"><sup>22</sup></xref></td><td align="center">2017</td><td align="center">China</td><td>Suspected myocarditis</td><td align="center">403</td><td align="center">33 &#x000b1; 14</td><td align="center">59.1</td><td align="center">9.7</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td></tr><tr><td>Younis et al<xref rid="bib23" ref-type="bibr"><sup>23</sup></xref></td><td align="center">2020</td><td align="center">Israel</td><td>Acute myocarditis</td><td align="center">322</td><td align="center">37 &#x000b1; 14</td><td align="center">84.0</td><td align="center">0.0</td><td align="center">3.0</td><td align="center">1 y</td><td align="center">0.0</td><td align="center">0.0</td><td align="center">0.0</td></tr><tr><td>Kanaoka et al<xref rid="bib24" ref-type="bibr"><sup>24</sup></xref></td><td align="center">2022</td><td align="center">Japan</td><td>Histologically proven fulminant myocarditis</td><td align="center">344</td><td align="center">54 (39-65)</td><td align="center">60.0</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">600 (36-1,599) d</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">33.1<xref rid="tbl1fna" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref></td></tr><tr><td>Hao et al<xref rid="bib25" ref-type="bibr"><sup>25</sup></xref></td><td align="center">2023</td><td align="center">China</td><td>Acute fulminant myocarditis supported with VA-ECMO</td><td align="center">221</td><td align="center">38 (29-49)</td><td align="center">52.0</td><td align="center">28.1</td><td align="center">89.1</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td></tr><tr><td>Kim et al<xref rid="bib13" ref-type="bibr"><sup>13</sup></xref></td><td align="center">2023</td><td align="center">Korea</td><td>Suspected acute de novo myocarditis</td><td align="center">2,988</td><td align="center">51 &#x000b1; 17</td><td align="center">53.8</td><td align="center">6.6 (30 d)</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">10 y</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">25.6<xref rid="tbl1fna" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref></td></tr><tr><td>Nasu et al<xref rid="bib26" ref-type="bibr"><sup>26</sup></xref></td><td align="center">2023</td><td align="center">Japan</td><td>Fulminant myocarditis</td><td align="center">269</td><td align="center">54 (39-67)</td><td align="center">57.2</td><td align="center">25.7 (30 d)</td><td align="center">85.3 (LVEF&#x0003c;40)</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td></tr><tr><td>Kanaoka et al<xref rid="bib27" ref-type="bibr"><sup>27</sup></xref></td><td align="center">2024</td><td align="center">Japan</td><td>Fulminant myocarditis discharged alive</td><td align="center">214</td><td align="center">48 (35-62)</td><td align="center">62.0</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">1,385 (508-1,841) d</td><td align="center">0.50</td><td align="center">3.3 (3-y)</td><td align="center">4.7</td></tr><tr><td>Bak et al<xref rid="bib28" ref-type="bibr"><sup>28</sup></xref></td><td align="center">2025</td><td align="center">Korea</td><td>Fulminant myocarditis who underwent VA-ECMO</td><td align="center">217</td><td align="center">39 (27-54)</td><td align="center">45.6</td><td align="center">30.4</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">26 (9-54) mo</td><td align="center">22.5<xref rid="tbl1fnb" ref-type="table-fn">b</xref></td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">3.3<xref rid="tbl1fna" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="tbl1fnc" ref-type="table-fn">c</xref></td></tr><tr><td>Kawai et al<xref rid="bib29" ref-type="bibr"><sup>29</sup></xref></td><td align="center">2025</td><td align="center">Japan</td><td>Histologically confirmed fulminant myocarditis</td><td align="center">344</td><td align="center">54 (median)</td><td align="center">60.0</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">3 y</td><td align="center">0.3</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">32.8<xref rid="tbl1fna" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref></td></tr><tr><td>Ueda et al<xref rid="bib30" ref-type="bibr"><sup>30</sup></xref></td><td align="center">2025</td><td align="center">Japan</td><td>Fulminant myocarditis</td><td align="center">343</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">58.0</td><td align="center">50.4</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">1 y</td><td align="center">0.0</td><td align="center">1.8</td><td align="center">2.9</td></tr><tr><td>Wang et al<xref rid="bib18" ref-type="bibr"><sup>18</sup></xref></td><td align="center">2025</td><td align="center">China</td><td>Acute myocarditis</td><td align="center">235</td><td align="center">32 &#x000b1; 13</td><td align="center">63.8</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">/</td><td align="center">1,637 (1,441-1,833) d</td><td align="center">0.9</td><td align="center">6.4</td><td align="center">4.7</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>ICU = intensive care unit; LVEF = left ventricular ejection fraction; VA-ECMO = venous-arterial extracorporeal membrane oxygenation.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tbl1fna"><label>a</label><p id="ntpara0010">All-cause mortality.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tbl1fnb"><label>b</label><p id="ntpara0015">These data were calculated with exclusion of in-hospital mortality and inclusion of patients who underwent heart transplantation or durable left ventricular assist device implantation.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tbl1fnc"><label>c</label><p id="ntpara0020">These data were calculated with exclusion of in-hospital mortality.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></p><p id="p0055">In addition to the data retrieved from the GBD database, there are also published studies from Asian countries investigating the epidemiology of myocarditis following the administration of the BNT162b2 vaccine. A study conducted in South Korea reported a higher incidence of myocarditis/pericarditis among individuals aged 12 to 19 years, with an incidence of 2.25 cases per 100,000 doses of the vaccine occurring within 42 days post-vaccination.<xref rid="bib31" ref-type="bibr"><sup>31</sup></xref> Similarly, a study in Malaysia evaluated the risk of myocarditis and pericarditis occurring 21 days after BNT162b2 vaccination in individuals aged 12 years and older, revealing an incidence of 1.73 cases of myocarditis/pericarditis per million doses administered, corresponding to an absolute risk of 0.33 cases per 100,000 vaccinated individuals.<xref rid="bib32" ref-type="bibr"><sup>32</sup></xref></p><p id="p0060">In summary, there are significant disparities in the incidence and mortality rates of myocarditis across Asia, with a particularly substantial burden observed among the young and middle-aged population (<xref rid="fig6" ref-type="fig">Central Illustration</xref>). These disparities reflect not only regional epidemiological characteristics but also highlight the varying impacts of health systems and capacity of public health care. This underscores the need for region-specific prevention strategies in the future.<fig id="fig6"><label>Central Illustration</label><caption><p>Epidemiology of Young and Middle-Aged Myocarditis in Asia</p><p>From 1990 to 2021, the numbers of incident cases and deaths of myocarditis in Asia exhibited an upward trend. In 2021, the young and middle-aged population accounted for 51.64% of the total incident cases and 25.85% of total myocarditis deaths in Asia. In addition, published papers indicated that young and middle-aged people were the predominant affected population, with male individuals comprising the majority.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="gr6"/></fig></p></sec><sec id="sec2"><title>Challenges in the Report of Epidemiological Data</title><p id="p0065">Although the GBD database provides a valuable source of epidemiological data on myocarditis, it is important to acknowledge that reports on epidemiological data of myocarditis in Asia face challenges rooted in both clinical underdiagnosis and systemic limitations in health care infrastructure, which collectively obscure the true disease burden. For instance, a survey in Singapore indicated that up to 38.5% of childhood myocarditis fatalities were incorrectly coded as respiratory tract infections,<xref rid="bib33" ref-type="bibr"><sup>33</sup></xref> as myocarditis often presents with nonspecific symptoms, such as fatigue, dyspnea, or vague chest discomfort.<xref rid="bib2" ref-type="bibr"><sup>2</sup></xref> In resource-constrained settings, where advanced diagnostic tools like cardiac magnetic resonance imaging (CMR) or endomyocardial biopsy are scarce, clinicians frequently rely on echocardiography or clinical suspicion alone. Studies in India revealed that up to 30% of pediatric myocarditis cases were often misclassified as rheumatic heart disease or other heart disease due to overlapping clinical features.<xref rid="bib34" ref-type="bibr"><sup>34</sup></xref> In addition, clinical studies on myocarditis in the Asian population are still relatively scarce compared with those conducted in the Western population. In Western studies, the reported recurrence rate of myocarditis ranged from 2.6% to 18.0%.<xref rid="bib35" ref-type="bibr">35</xref>, <xref rid="bib36" ref-type="bibr">36</xref>, <xref rid="bib37" ref-type="bibr">37</xref>, <xref rid="bib38" ref-type="bibr">38</xref> However, there is a notable lack of epidemiological data regarding the recurrence rate of myocarditis in Asia, despite a few case reports documenting instances of myocarditis recurrence and its management. Further efforts and studies are necessary to address these gaps and enhance our understanding of the hidden burden of myocarditis in Asia.</p></sec><sec id="sec3"><title>Etiologies in the Asian Population</title><p id="p0070">Viral infections are the predominant cause of myocarditis.<xref rid="bib39" ref-type="bibr"><sup>39</sup></xref> The most frequently identified viral pathogens are enteroviruses, particularly Coxsackievirus B3, which is associated with both acute myocarditis and the progression to dilated cardiomyopathy due to persistent immune-mediated damage.<xref rid="bib40" ref-type="bibr"><sup>40</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib41" ref-type="bibr"><sup>41</sup></xref> However, regional variations in viral pathogens in Asia are influenced by genetic polymorphisms within the population, endemic viral strains, health care infrastructure, and diagnostic practices. An Asian cohort study showed that the genetic polymorphisms in innate immune sensors, such as Toll-like receptors, could modulate host susceptibility to viral persistence and autoimmune transition.<xref rid="bib42" ref-type="bibr"><sup>42</sup></xref> Furthermore, emerging viruses, such as dengue, exhibit geographically skewed prevalence. Dengue-associated myocarditis shows higher incidence in tropical Southeast Asian countries like Thailand and Indonesia, correlating with endemic mosquito-borne transmission cycles.<xref rid="bib43" ref-type="bibr"><sup>43</sup></xref></p><p id="p0075">In recent years, other emerging etiological factors, COVID-19&#x02013;associated myocarditis and post-vaccination myocarditis, also reshape the disease landscape. Studies have indicated that SARS-CoV-2 infection significantly increased the risk of myocarditis,<xref rid="bib44" ref-type="bibr"><sup>44</sup></xref> with COVID-19&#x02013;related myocardial inflammation showing high rates of subclinical myocarditis detected via CMR in convalescent patients, as reported in Asian cohorts.<xref rid="bib45" ref-type="bibr"><sup>45</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib46" ref-type="bibr"><sup>46</sup></xref> One study reported that acute myocarditis induced by COVID-19 predominantly affected younger adults (median age 37 years), with 25% developing fulminant courses requiring mechanical circulatory support.<xref rid="bib47" ref-type="bibr"><sup>47</sup></xref> Moreover, the pandemic has exacerbated trends in cardiovascular mortality, driven by indirect effects from health care disruptions and delays in the treatment of acute myocarditis.<xref rid="bib48" ref-type="bibr"><sup>48</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib49" ref-type="bibr"><sup>49</sup></xref> Although rare, post-vaccination associated with messenger RNA COVID-19 vaccines, has also been documented in the Asian population, with studies from Japan and South Korea showing a predominant impact on male individuals younger than 30 years.<xref rid="bib50" ref-type="bibr"><sup>50</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib51" ref-type="bibr"><sup>51</sup></xref> However, a study showed that the Southeast Asian population exhibited a lower proportion of vaccine-associated myocarditis/pericarditis (0.08%) compared with the European region (32.94%).<xref rid="bib52" ref-type="bibr"><sup>52</sup></xref></p><p id="p0080">For the noninfectious etiology, autoimmune-mediated myocarditis represents a significant subset, with systemic lupus erythematosus being a prominent contributor in East Asia due to its higher prevalence compared with the Western population.<xref rid="bib53" ref-type="bibr"><sup>53</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib54" ref-type="bibr"><sup>54</sup></xref> Its pathogenesis involves the perpetuation of regulated T-cell responses and cytokine cascades.<xref rid="bib55" ref-type="bibr"><sup>55</sup></xref> This autoimmune propensity may be exacerbated by regional genetic factors, such as the association of HLA-DR2 with the susceptibility to systemic lupus erythematosus, as reported in a cohort from China.<xref rid="bib56" ref-type="bibr"><sup>56</sup></xref> Moreover, advanced therapies, such as immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs), also have been frequently reported to induce myocarditis in the Asian population.<xref rid="bib57" ref-type="bibr"><sup>57</sup></xref></p></sec><sec id="sec4"><title>Diagnostic Challenges and Clinical Practice in Asia</title><p id="p0085">The diagnostic challenges of myocarditis arise from its nonspecific symptoms, such as chest pain and fatigue, which are further complicated when considering differential diagnoses in the context of COVID-19.<xref rid="bib58" ref-type="bibr"><sup>58</sup></xref> The overlap in symptoms can lead to misdiagnosis or delayed diagnosis, potentially resulting in severe consequences. Countries with robust public health infrastructures, such as China and Japan, have been more proactive in identifying myocarditis cases associated with infections. In contrast, less developed nations may struggle with accurate diagnosis due to limited access to CMR and other specialized diagnostic modalities.<xref rid="bib59" ref-type="bibr"><sup>59</sup></xref></p><p id="p0090">For the diagnosis of myocarditis, European studies have reported a high positive predictive value and specificity when using CMR,<xref rid="bib60" ref-type="bibr"><sup>60</sup></xref> establishing it as the cornerstone for noninvasive detection of myocardial inflammation.<xref rid="bib61" ref-type="bibr"><sup>61</sup></xref> Similarly, diagnostic practices in Asia are increasingly incorporating CMR for noninvasive assessment, aligning with global trends.<xref rid="bib62" ref-type="bibr"><sup>62</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib63" ref-type="bibr"><sup>63</sup></xref> Other noninvasive methods also have been explored in Asia. A study from China highlighted that echocardiography-derived global longitudinal strain could serve as a surrogate for monitoring the myocardial fibrosis and edema during the course of myocarditis.<xref rid="bib64" ref-type="bibr"><sup>64</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib65" ref-type="bibr"><sup>65</sup></xref> However, for an invasive technique, endomyocardial biopsy remains underutilized in many Asian regions due to procedural risk and limited access.<xref rid="bib66" ref-type="bibr"><sup>66</sup></xref></p><p id="p0095">The application of laboratory testing is also frequently used in the diagnosis and risk stratification of myocarditis. In the Asian population, conventional biomarkers such as cardiac troponins (cTnI, cTnT) and creatine kinase isoenzyme-MB remain central to the evaluation of myocarditis.<xref rid="bib67" ref-type="bibr"><sup>67</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib68" ref-type="bibr"><sup>68</sup></xref> A study from the Asian population showed that elevated cTnI levels were strongly associated with myocarditis severity, with higher cTnI levels observed in patients with myocarditis compared with those with arrhythmias or heart failure (<italic>P</italic>&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.001).<xref rid="bib68" ref-type="bibr"><sup>68</sup></xref> In addition to the biomarkers related to myocardial injury, Japanese research identified novel autoantibody profiles as biomarkers for ICI-associated myocarditis.<xref rid="bib69" ref-type="bibr"><sup>69</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib70" ref-type="bibr"><sup>70</sup></xref> Other Asian studies also came up with the utility of inflammatory ratios such as the systemic immune-inflammation index, albumin-to&#x02013;red cell distribution width ratio, neutrophil-to-lymphocyte ratio, and neutrophil-to-eosinophil ratio for risk prediction in the ICI-associated myocarditis.<xref rid="bib68" ref-type="bibr"><sup>68</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib71" ref-type="bibr"><sup>71</sup></xref> These biomarkers, which integrate hematological and inflammatory parameters, demonstrated superior diagnostic value in Asian cohorts. Specifically, a systemic immune-inflammation index &#x02265;1,020 showed 91% sensitivity and 83% specificity for diagnosing fulminant myocarditis in young adults, outperforming traditional troponins.<xref rid="bib72" ref-type="bibr"><sup>72</sup></xref> A study conducted in China also showed that parameters like soluble suppression of tumorigenicity-2 (sST2) levels had a sensitivity of 85.7% and specificity of 94.7% in diagnosing fulminant myocarditis.<xref rid="bib73" ref-type="bibr"><sup>73</sup></xref> In the future, it is hoped that more diagnostic models will be developed to collectively improve the diagnosis of myocarditis.</p></sec><sec id="sec5"><title>Prognosis and Treatment Strategies in Asia</title><sec id="sec5.1"><title>Prognosis</title><p id="p0100">Several studies have suggested that mild myocarditis cases in the young and middle-aged population may follow a self-limiting course.<xref rid="bib74" ref-type="bibr"><sup>74</sup></xref> A population-based study conducted in China further revealed that acute myocarditis severity did not predict long-term outcomes in adults, although early cardiovascular deaths (within 3 months) correlated with initial disease severity.<xref rid="bib21" ref-type="bibr"><sup>21</sup></xref> This review summarizes relevant published original research investigating the prognosis of myocarditis in Asian populations, as presented in <xref rid="tbl1" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref>. The findings showed that, although there were no in-hospital deaths reported in an Israeli study, which may be due to the inclusion of relatively less severe cases, studies in China, Japan, and Korea indicated high in-hospital mortality rates for fulminant myocarditis, the most severe form, ranging from 25.7% to 50.4%, with a heart failure rate of approximately 85%. In terms of long-term outcomes, patients with acute myocarditis faced a risk of up to 8.2% of developing heart failure. In addition, a Japanese study reported an all-cause mortality rate of up to 33.1% in patients with histologically proven fulminant myocarditis during a follow-up period of 600 (36-1,599) days.</p><p id="p0105">In addition, several studies have identified new biomarkers for predicting the prognosis of myocarditis. One study conducted in an Asian population highlighted the prognostic significance of hemoglobin/red blood cell distribution width ratio and red blood cell distribution width/albumin ratio, which correlated with disease severity and survival outcomes in patients with myocarditis.<xref rid="bib75" ref-type="bibr"><sup>75</sup></xref> Another study performed in pediatric myocarditis demonstrated that N-terminal pro&#x02013;B-type natriuretic peptide and sST2 showed great performance in predicting major adverse cardiac events, with elevated N-terminal pro&#x02013;B-type natriuretic peptide (OR: 4.32) and sST2 (OR: 1.02) independently associated with hospitalization outcomes.<xref rid="bib76" ref-type="bibr"><sup>76</sup></xref> Furthermore, a study found that the acute kidney injury network classification for acute kidney injury and sequential organ failure assessment scores could effectively stratify mortality risk in acute myocarditis, with acute kidney injury network stage 3 and elevated sequential organ failure assessment scores correlating with poor outcomes.<xref rid="bib77" ref-type="bibr"><sup>77</sup></xref> These tools, validated in the Asian population, underscore the interplay between multi-organ dysfunction and myocarditis-related mortality.</p><p id="p0110">Electrocardiographic patterns also have been shown to predict the course of myocarditis. For instance, early repolarization, which was present in 30% of Japanese patients with acute myocarditis, was associated with lower troponin levels and a benign clinical course. In contrast, non&#x02013;early repolarization patterns, such as ST-segment elevation, were indicative of severe myocardial injury.<xref rid="bib78" ref-type="bibr"><sup>78</sup></xref> In addition, a separate analysis from Japan indicated that systolic hypotension (&#x0003c;101 mm Hg) and QRS prolongation (&#x0003e;120 ms) at admission were strong predictors of fulminant courses.<xref rid="bib79" ref-type="bibr"><sup>79</sup></xref> Together with these indices, Asian prognostic models could be further optimized.</p></sec><sec id="sec5.2"><title>Treatment strategies</title><p id="p0115">There are significant disparities in the management of fulminant myocarditis, the most severe form of myocarditis, between Western populations and those in Asia. In Western populations, fulminant myocarditis was associated with a high mortality, reaching up to 50% to 70%.<xref rid="bib80" ref-type="bibr"><sup>80</sup></xref> In Asia, the management of fulminant myocarditis frequently incorporates intravenous immunoglobulin, a practice less emphasized in Western guidelines.<xref rid="bib81" ref-type="bibr"><sup>81</sup></xref> In China, a life support&#x02013;based comprehensive treatment regimen that emphasized early application of mechanical life support, immunomodulation, and neuraminidase inhibitors for fulminant myocarditis had decreased the mortality rate to 3.7%.<xref rid="bib82" ref-type="bibr"><sup>82</sup></xref> In Japan, a multicenter retrospective study of patients with fulminant myocarditis revealed complete left ventricular ejection fraction recovery in 86% of survivors at 1-year follow-up after treatment with intravenous immunoglobulin and steroids.<xref rid="bib27" ref-type="bibr"><sup>27</sup></xref> In addition, lupus myocarditis in Asian cohorts had shown favorable recovery of left ventricular function with high-dose corticosteroids.<xref rid="bib83" ref-type="bibr"><sup>83</sup></xref> Furthermore, during the Omicron wave, COVID-19&#x02013;related myocarditis was shown to benefit from the administration of angiotensin II receptor type 1 blockers.<xref rid="bib84" ref-type="bibr"><sup>84</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib85" ref-type="bibr"><sup>85</sup></xref></p><p id="p0120">Moreover, Asian countries have increasingly integrated traditional medicine, such as <italic>Astragalus membranaceus</italic> injections, into conventional supportive care for viral myocarditis, with some trials reporting improved outcomes in heart function and symptom alleviation.<xref rid="bib86" ref-type="bibr"><sup>86</sup></xref> However, this finding is tempered by methodological limitations, including small sample sizes and high risk of bias, underscoring the need for rigorous multicenter studies. In <xref rid="tbl2" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref>, we have listed all registered studies involving the Asian population on clinical trial websites.<xref rid="bib87" ref-type="bibr"><sup>87</sup></xref> Most of these studies are still ongoing. Several promising drugs for the treatment of myocarditis, such as hydroxychloroquine and tofacitinib, hold potential for future use. The implementation of these clinical studies is expected to optimize therapeutic strategies for this patient demographic in the future.<table-wrap position="float" id="tbl2"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Summary of Studies on Myocarditis in the Young and Middle-Aged Asian Population</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th>NCT Number</th><th>Study Region</th><th>Study Title</th><th>Study Population</th><th>Sample Size</th><th>Study Type</th><th>Start Date</th><th>Completion Date</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT03219996" id="intref0025">NCT03219996</ext-link></td><td align="center">China</td><td>Risk Factors for Predictors of In-hospital Death in Acute Fulminant Myocarditis</td><td>Acute fulminant myocarditis</td><td align="center">76</td><td align="center">Observational</td><td align="center">1/1/2016</td><td align="center">12/30/2016</td></tr><tr><td><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT03268642" id="intref0030">NCT03268642</ext-link></td><td align="center">China</td><td>Clinical Assessment of New Treatment Regimen for Adult Fulminant Myocarditis</td><td>Fulminant myocarditis</td><td align="center">150</td><td align="center">Observational</td><td align="center">2/1/2016</td><td align="center">12/2021</td></tr><tr><td><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT03607071" id="intref0035">NCT03607071</ext-link></td><td align="center">Thailand</td><td>Outcome of Steroid Therapy for Myocardial Inflammation in Scleroderma</td><td>Scleroderma and myocarditis</td><td align="center">20</td><td align="center">Interventional</td><td align="center">5/15/2018</td><td align="center">6/30/2019</td></tr><tr><td><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT04498494" id="intref0040">NCT04498494</ext-link></td><td align="center">China</td><td>The Role of Electrocardiograms in Risk Stratification and Analysis of the Characteristics of ST Elevation in Acute Myocarditis: a 2-centre Study</td><td>Acute myocarditis</td><td align="center">274</td><td align="center">Observational</td><td align="center">1/1/2019</td><td align="center">1/1/2020</td></tr><tr><td><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT04304118" id="intref0045">NCT04304118</ext-link></td><td align="center">Iran</td><td>Rajaie Cardiomyopathy and Myocarditis Registry</td><td>Myocarditis/Cardiomyopathy</td><td align="center">6,000</td><td align="center">Observational</td><td align="center">11/11/2019</td><td align="center">11/11/2025</td></tr><tr><td><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT04338113" id="intref0050">NCT04338113</ext-link></td><td align="center">China</td><td>EARLY-MYO-Myocarditis Registry</td><td>Myocarditis</td><td align="center">200</td><td align="center">Observational</td><td align="center">1/1/2020</td><td align="center">1/1/2025</td></tr><tr><td><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT05718128" id="intref0055">NCT05718128</ext-link></td><td align="center">China</td><td>Clinical Study of Endocardial Myocardial Biopsy</td><td>Myocarditis/Cardiomyopathies/Heart failure/Cardiac tumor</td><td align="center">100</td><td align="center">Interventional</td><td align="center">6/1/2021</td><td align="center">6/1/2031</td></tr><tr><td><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT05288426" id="intref0060">NCT05288426</ext-link></td><td align="center">Israel</td><td>Myocarditis Post Vaccination or Multi System Inflammatory Syndrome</td><td>Myocarditis</td><td align="center">150</td><td align="center">Observational</td><td align="center">11/1/2021</td><td align="center">12/1/2025</td></tr><tr><td><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT05282498" id="intref0065">NCT05282498</ext-link></td><td align="center">Singapore</td><td>Pathophysiologic Mechanism for MYOcarditis in COVID19 VAccinations ("MYOVAx" Study)</td><td>Myocarditis</td><td align="center">50</td><td align="center">Observational</td><td align="center">11/1/2021</td><td align="center">12/31/2023</td></tr><tr><td><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT05961202" id="intref0070">NCT05961202</ext-link></td><td align="center">China</td><td>The Effects of Hydroxychloroquine in Patients with Inflammatory Cardiomyopathy</td><td>Chronic myocarditis</td><td align="center">200</td><td align="center">Interventional</td><td align="center">11/1/2021</td><td align="center">11/1/2025</td></tr><tr><td><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT05288231" id="intref0075">NCT05288231</ext-link></td><td align="center">Thailand</td><td>Cardiac Manifestation in Adolescent After Pfizer COVID-19 Injection in Thailand</td><td>Covid-19 vaccine</td><td align="center">314</td><td align="center">Observational</td><td align="center">11/3/2021</td><td align="center">3/15/2022</td></tr><tr><td><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT05933902" id="intref0080">NCT05933902</ext-link></td><td align="center">Korea</td><td>Multicenter Retrospective Observational Study of Acute Myocarditis in Korea</td><td>Acute myocarditis</td><td align="center">841</td><td align="center">Observational</td><td align="center">4/12/2022</td><td align="center">1/25/2023</td></tr><tr><td><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT06010199" id="intref0085">NCT06010199</ext-link></td><td align="center">China</td><td>Risk Stratification Value of Biomarkers in Patients With Myocarditis</td><td>Myocarditis</td><td align="center">1,000</td><td align="center">Observational</td><td align="center">8/25/2023</td><td align="center">8/31/2030</td></tr><tr><td><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT06393972" id="intref0090">NCT06393972</ext-link></td><td align="center">China</td><td>The Efficacy and Safety of Tofacitinib in Patients With Glucocorticoid Resistant ICIs-related Myocarditis</td><td>Glucocorticoid resistant ICI-related myocarditis</td><td align="center">25</td><td align="center">Interventional</td><td align="center">7/1/2024</td><td align="center">11/30/2026</td></tr><tr><td><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT06742008" id="intref0095">NCT06742008</ext-link></td><td align="center">China</td><td>Assessment of the Predictive Value of Anti-beta1AR and Anti-L-CaC Antibodies in the Evolution to Dilated Cardiomyopathy in Patients With Acute Viral Myocarditis</td><td>Acute viral myocarditis</td><td align="center">300</td><td align="center">Observational</td><td align="center">12/17/2024</td><td align="center">6/17/2025</td></tr><tr><td><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT06818149" id="intref0100">NCT06818149</ext-link></td><td align="center">China</td><td>Biomarkers for Clinical Classification and Outcomes of Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor-Related-Related Myocarditis in Lung Cancer</td><td>ICI-related myocarditis</td><td align="center">50</td><td align="center">Observational</td><td align="center">1/30/2025</td><td align="center">12/30/2028</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>ICI = immune checkpoint inhibitor.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></p></sec></sec><sec id="sec6"><title>Public Health Management in Asia</title><sec id="sec6.1"><title>The shortcomings of public health care</title><p id="p0125">The epidemiology of myocarditis exhibits regional variations across Asia, highlighting the necessity for tailored public health interventions to address this health burden. For example, in China, the age-standardized incidence rate decreased by 6.06%, and the age-standardized mortality rate declined by 16.04% from 1990 to 2019, reflecting improvements in health care.<xref rid="bib88" ref-type="bibr"><sup>88</sup></xref> However, persistent gender disparities existed, with higher age-standardized incidence and mortality rates in male compared with female individuals.<xref rid="bib88" ref-type="bibr"><sup>88</sup></xref> Moreover, the age distribution of myocarditis-related morbidity and mortality has shifted toward older groups, suggesting improved survival rates among younger individuals but changes in risk factor profiles.<xref rid="bib88" ref-type="bibr"><sup>88</sup></xref> Besides, delayed recognition of myocarditis is also a significant issue in China, contributing to its prominence as a cause of sudden cardiac death, which accounts for 34.7% of sudden cardiac death cases in individuals younger than 35 years, a rate higher than that observed in Western countries.<xref rid="bib89" ref-type="bibr"><sup>89</sup></xref> In addition, Japanese pharmacovigilance data revealed that myocarditis was the most common cardiac complication following ICI therapy, with potential cardiac complications arising at a median onset of 33 days post treatment, underscoring the need for vigilant monitoring during the ICI therapies.<xref rid="bib90" ref-type="bibr"><sup>90</sup></xref> In tropical regions of Asia, infectious etiologies remain prominent, necessitating enhanced health care surveillance of viral pathogens.<xref rid="bib91" ref-type="bibr"><sup>91</sup></xref></p><p id="p0130">Furthermore, access to advanced therapies varies widely. In high-income Asian countries, left ventricular assist devices (LVADs) and heart transplantation programs have improved outcomes for myocarditis-induced heart failure. For instance, patients in Singapore with myocarditis-related cardiomyopathy achieved functional recovery or successful bridging to transplantation using LVADs.<xref rid="bib92" ref-type="bibr"><sup>92</sup></xref> However, in resource-limited Asian regions, limited availability of transplantation services and LVADs forces reliance on suboptimal surgical interventions, such as left ventricular volume reduction, which yield disappointing survival rates (65.2% at 18 months).<xref rid="bib93" ref-type="bibr"><sup>93</sup></xref></p></sec><sec id="sec6.2"><title>The possible solutions</title><p id="p0135">Strengthening the recognition of myocarditis and integrating biomarkers, such as troponin, sST2, and interleukin-17A into diagnostic protocols may improve early detection.<xref rid="bib94" ref-type="bibr"><sup>94</sup></xref> Furthermore, investigating genetic predispositions, such as interleukin-17A polymorphisms (eg, rs2275913) associated with the severity of myocarditis, could enable personalized risk stratification and targeted therapies.<xref rid="bib94" ref-type="bibr"><sup>94</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib95" ref-type="bibr"><sup>95</sup></xref> In addition, promoting the use of CMR in underserved areas through cost-reduction initiatives and training programs is critical. In regions with high infectious disease burdens, vaccination campaigns targeting enteroviruses, dengue, and leptospirosis may help reduce the incidence of myocarditis. Moreover, countries that use immunotherapies or clozapine should adopt stricter monitoring protocols. Similarly, Asian guidelines for managing ICI-related myocarditis should prioritize longitudinal cardiac monitoring, even beyond the first year of treatment.<xref rid="bib90" ref-type="bibr"><sup>90</sup></xref> Last, training health care providers in myocarditis management and improving referral networks may help reduce disparities in health care quality.</p></sec></sec><sec id="sec7"><title>Perspectives and Conclusions</title><p id="p0140">Myocarditis represents a significant public health concern in Asia, particularly among the young and middle-aged population. Clinically, severe manifestations of myocarditis, including fulminant heart failure, cardiogenic shock, or sudden cardiac death, are disproportionately observed in these age groups, contributing to its recognition as a predominant cause of death.<xref rid="bib96" ref-type="bibr"><sup>96</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib97" ref-type="bibr"><sup>97</sup></xref> In terms of the long-term sequelae, up to one-third of myocarditis cases progress to dilated cardiomyopathy and chronic heart failure, emphasizing the need for early intervention and longitudinal monitoring.<xref rid="bib98" ref-type="bibr"><sup>98</sup></xref> In addition, the diagnostic challenges associated with myocarditis are exacerbated by the heterogeneity of presentations and the limited use of endomyocardial biopsy, the gold standard for confirmation, due to its invasiveness and accessibility issues.<xref rid="bib99" ref-type="bibr"><sup>99</sup></xref> Advanced imaging modalities, particularly CMR, should be advocated to enhance the noninvasive detection of myocardial inflammation and fibrosis,<xref rid="bib63" ref-type="bibr"><sup>63</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib100" ref-type="bibr"><sup>100</sup></xref> alongside collaborative efforts to standardize diagnostic criteria. Moreover, exploring the interplay among genetic predisposition, environmental factors, and immune responses in Asian cohorts may also unveil novel therapeutic targets in the future.</p><p id="p0145">Currently, epidemiological data specific to Asian cohorts remain sparse, and the true data on epidemiology of myocarditis in Asia is likely underdiagnosed and underreported. Nevertheless, existing literature highlights critical regional variability and unmet health care needs. This pattern underscores the influence of socioeconomic factors and health care infrastructure on disease burden. Addressing these issues requires a multifaceted approach that includes pathogen control, pharmacovigilance, and infrastructure investment. More importantly, future research must prioritize large-scale population-based studies to delineate the incidence, etiology, and outcomes of myocarditis across Asian subregions. By integrating these strategies, the Asian population may mitigate the burden of myocarditis in young and middle-aged adults, aligning with global efforts to reduce cardiovascular morbidity and mortality.</p></sec><sec id="sec88"><title>Conclusions</title><p id="p0150">Myocarditis in the young and middle-aged Asian population shares broad pathophysiological features similar to global patterns, regional nuances in etiology, clinical profiles, and health care challenges require prioritized attention. Bridging these knowledge gaps through targeted research and equitable resource allocation will be pivotal in improving management for this vulnerable population.</p></sec><sec sec-type="COI-statement" id="coi0010"><title>Funding Support and Author Disclosures</title><p id="p0160">This work was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (no. 82100403). The authors have reported that they have no relationships relevant to the contents of this paper to disclose.</p></sec></body><back><ref-list id="cebib0010"><title>References</title><ref id="bib1"><label>1</label><element-citation publicati